Dialogue History:
Assistant: What are the primary business outcomes and success criteria you want this supply chain management system to achieve for Ejada?
User: The main outcome we want is better end-to-end visibility and control across procurement, delivery, and service execution so we can respond faster and reduce delays. We also want clearer cost management, stronger supplier performance, and better coordination between teams so our projects and customer commitments are met more reliably. Success would mean fewer stock or delivery issues, improved productivity, more accurate planning, and better service quality that we can measure across the supply chain.
Assistant: Which stakeholder roles will use or be affected by the system, and what responsibilities or decisions should each role have across procurement, delivery, service execution, and performance monitoring?
User: The main users will be procurement staff, supply chain managers, warehouse or logistics staff, project or service delivery teams, finance or operations management, and probably supplier partners in some limited way. Procurement staff would handle supplier selection, purchase requests, and order follow-up, while delivery and warehouse teams would manage receiving, dispatching, and tracking materials. Service delivery teams would use it to coordinate product or service execution, and managers would review performance, costs, delays, and quality so they can approve decisions and intervene when needed.

